      "reasoning": "The user wants me to make a billiards (pool) game. This is a self-contained task — I should create an HTML/CSS/JS game as a single .html file. Let me think about where to save it. The task place is /BASE/direction/sales «Продажи» [$group]. That's a sales department, a bit odd for a game, but that's the task place. I'll save it there.\n\nLet me build a proper 8-ball pool game in a single HTML file. I'll make it reasonably complete:\n- Table with cushions, pockets\n- Cue ball + 15 balls (solids 1-7, 8-ball, stripes 9-15)\n- Aiming with mouse, power control\n- Physics: ball-ball collisions, ball-cushion collisions, friction\n- Pocket detection\n- Turn-based two players, simple rules (solids vs stripes, 8-ball win/loss)\n- Score display\n\nI'll write it in chunks since it's large. Let me plan the structure:\n\n1. HTML skeleton + canvas + UI (score, turn, power bar)\n2. CSS\n3. JS: constants, ball setup, physics loop, input handling, game logic\n\nLet me write it carefully. I'll use a canvas-based approach.\n\nLet me think about physics:\n- Each ball: x, y, vx, vy, radius, color, number, type (solid/stripe/8/cue)\n- Friction: multiply velocity by ~0.99 each frame, stop below threshold\n- Ball-ball collision: elastic, equal mass\n- Cushion collision: reflect velocity with some damping\n- Pockets: 6 pockets at corners and mid-sides; if ball center within pocket radius, remove ball\n\nAiming:\n- Mouse position relative to cue ball defines direction\n- Click and drag to set power, or use a power bar. I'll do: move mouse to aim (line from cue ball toward mouse), hold mouse button to charge power, release to shoot. Or simpler: click to set direction, then a power slider. Let me do drag-to-shoot: press on cue ball area, drag back to set power and direction, release to fire. Actually a common approach: aim with mouse position (line shows direction), then click-hold to increase power (oscillating or filling bar), release to shoot.\n\nLet me do: mouse move aims